INT. NIGHT FERRY GALLEY - NIGHT

A cramped steel galley, steam rising from a stockpot, a single porthole showing black water and distant harbor lights.

CELESTE ARMITAGE, thirties, a pastry chef in a stained white jacket, leans against the counter, arms folded. DOMINIC VARGA, thirties, a passenger in a soaked grey coat, stands in the doorway holding a violin case. CAPTAIN OYELARAN, sixties, silver-bearded, pours coffee into three mugs and hands one to Celeste, one to Dominic, and keeps one. DECKHAND SUNNY, twenties, wipes down the counter, glancing from Celeste to Dominic and back with obvious amusement.

HARBORMASTER (O.S.)
(over radio)
Ferry Marisol, this is Harbor. Dock three is clear. Over.

CAPTAIN OYELARAN
(into radio)
Harbor, Marisol. Ten minutes out. Over.

Dominic sets down the violin case. Celeste picks up a whisk and puts it down. He crosses the galley, stops a foot from her, and neither of them says anything while the captain sips and Sunny bites his lip.

CELESTE
You said you'd never take this boat again.

DOMINIC
I said a lot of things in that rain.

HARBORMASTER (O.S.)
(over radio)
Marisol, confirm passenger count. Over.

CAPTAIN OYELARAN
(into radio)
Harbor, Marisol. Count is... flexible. Over.

Celeste laughs before she can stop it. Dominic smiles. Sunny drops the rag.

DOMINIC
I wrote you forty letters.

CELESTE
I read thirty-nine.

DOMINIC
Which one did you skip?

CELESTE
The one where you said goodbye.

The ferry horn blares. Dominic takes her hand, and she lets him, and the captain turns his back to study the porthole with enormous interest, Sunny scrambling after him with the mugs.
